  "worked_solution": "Let's denote by $A$ and $B$ the following statements:\n\n- $A:$ You exercise regularly.\n\n- $B:$ You will improve your health.\n\nThen, our initial statement can be written as $A\u21d2B,$ which is logically equivalent to \n\n$$\n\u00acA\u2228B.\n$$\n\nThe negation of $A$ can be written as\n\n- $\u00acA:$ You do not exercise regularly.\n\nTherefore, the given statement is logically equivalent to the following:\n\n$\\,$ You do not exercise regularly, or you will improve your health.\n\nWe can write this in more everyday language as follows:\n\n$\\,$ Either you do not exercise regularly, or you'll improve your health.\n\n**Note**: \n\nUsing the word \"Either\" helps clarify that the sentence structure presents two alternatives and is particularly useful when employing the disjunctive form $(\u00acP\u2228Q)$ in conversational or formal English. So, the phrase effectively conveys that one of the two stated conditions will hold true.",
